+/// The receiver one array store's access reads, widened to `Object[]` when the store is the
+/// **covariant** shape: a reference component the stored value's own type is not proven to meet.
+///
+/// A store is the one position where the closed reference-compatibility rule this layer already
+/// states ([`array_initializer_element`]) can leave text `javac` refuses. Arrays are covariant
+/// (JLS 4.10.3), so the source's own receiver may have stated a **wider** component than the array
+/// the value holds, while the presentation declares the receiver with the array its own descriptor
+/// or its creation states — `Object[] a = new String[2]; a[0] = Integer.valueOf(1);` presents
+/// `java.lang.String[] local0`, and `local0[0] = java.lang.Integer.valueOf(1);` is not a program,
+/// even though the bytecode's `aastore` is one. Which class is assignable to which is a subtype
+/// judgment this layer deliberately does not make (`make-required-conversions-explicit`'s
+/// non-goal), so the proven compatible cases are the initializer rule's own three — the exact
+/// component type, an `Object` component and a `null` value — and every other reference value
+/// widens the receiver.
+///
+/// `((Object[]) receiver)[index] = value` changes neither evaluation nor the runtime check: the
+/// cast applies to the receiver expression, which is evaluated exactly once; every reference array
+/// is a subtype of `Object[]`, so the cast cannot fail and performs no check of its own; and the
+/// `aastore` the bytecode runs still checks the value against the array's **runtime** component, so
+/// the `ArrayStoreException` a covariant store throws stays exactly where the class file throws it.
+/// The receiver is not re-declared: this is the access's own presentation, and the local keeps the
+/// array its own facts state.
+///
+/// A primitive component never widens: primitives have no covariant arrays, and the store's own
+/// conversion rules ([`meeting_position`], [`cast_argument`]) already own that side.
+fn widen_covariant_store_receiver(array: Expr, component: &Type, value: &Expr) -> Expr {
+    let Type::Reference(component) = component else {
+        return array;
+    };
+    if matches!(component.as_str(), "Object" | "java.lang.Object") {
+        return array;
+    }
+    // A `null`, a lambda and a method reference state no type at all: no mismatch is provable, so
+    // the value is written as it is — the rule every other position of this layer keeps.
+    let Some(Type::Reference(presented)) = value.presented.as_ref() else {
+        return array;
+    };
+    if presented == component {
+        return array;
+    }
+    let origin = array.origin.clone();
+    Expr::new(
+        ExprKind::Cast {
+            ty: Type::Reference("java.lang.Object[]".to_owned()),
+            value: Box::new(array),
+        },
+        origin,
+    )
+}
